WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:07.382
There is this big global industry

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:12.382
devoted to taking waste from
generally developed countries

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:15.306 position:56%
and shipping it to poorer countries,

00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:16.850
many of them in Southeast Asia.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:23.273 position:66%
Environmentalists have come up with a term

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:25.191
to describe this phenomenon,

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.653
and what they call it
is "waste colonialism."

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:30.822
The idea that rich countries

00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:34.576
are dumping their problems,
literally in some cases,

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:35.785
onto poor countries

00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:37.996
and letting poorer countries deal

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:41.416 position:64%
with the waste created in the rich world.

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:47.797
I went to Thailand hoping
to see some of that process

00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:49.924 position:64%
and the problems it's causing first-hand.

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:54.304
You know, I've been looking
at the data and at numbers,

00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:58.266
we've realized that a
tremendous amount of waste

00:00:58.266 --> 00:01:01.394
are being, well I don't want
to use the word imported,

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.856
I want to use the word dumping,
dumped into our country.

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:07.817
I know before I got
involved in this research

00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:11.780
what I thought about recycling
was wildly out of step

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.740
with the realities of the industry.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:16.409
I cannot sleep here, right?

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.452
Oh, I see.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:20.121
I come here and I open the house,

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:23.875
and inside the house has a
strong smell of the chemistry.

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.505
They can put up many, many plants.

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:29.756
They were planning for one,

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.967
but they told us that there
will be 13 more coming.

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:36.805 position:56%
Thailand is not the global dumpster.

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:39.557 position:66%
We are not an international bin, you know?

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:42.060
So if you don't have
anywhere to put your rubbish,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.937
please don't put it in Thailand.

00:01:56.699 --> 00:02:01.699
In 2021, Thailand received
about 158,000 tons

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:05.125 position:63%
of imported plastic waste from overseas.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:08.002 position:61%
So that is a hell of a lot of material.

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:12.715
It obviously needs to be
processed. It needs to be recycled.

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:14.926
Not all of it can be.

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:17.846
And it may also cause externalities

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:22.308 position:59%
like pollution, like noise, like fumes

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.018
that bother local residents.

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:27.689
So while we've all been
raised to think of recycling

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:31.067
as this kind of clean
and virtuous business,

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:32.902
this perfect circle,

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:36.906
actually there are big
problems with the process.

00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:39.367
And so the factory's right here.

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:41.536
Where would the fumes
come from? From this side?

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:43.163
From this hole and this hole.

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:45.415
So for this story

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:47.208 position:59%
one of the people I spoke to was a man

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:49.002
named Vinyou Jiaramankong.

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:51.296
Vinyou is an accountant.

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:54.591 position:59%
He has a house in the Bangkok suburbs.

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:56.718
And a couple years ago

00:02:56.718 --> 00:03:00.054
he found out that a
property next to his house,

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:03.349
which had previously been used
of a sort of storage yard,

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.895 position:63%
was being turned into a recycling plant.

00:03:06.895 --> 00:03:08.563
The whole area of this.

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:09.606
Was covered in smoke.

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:10.857
Yes, covered by smoke.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.775 position:58%
And that's why you have this big fan.

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:13.610
Yes.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.904
So after this plant got going,

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:20.450
Vinyou noticed that there
were really noxious fumes

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.118
coming into his home.

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:23.620
You cannot breathe.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:24.621
You have a headache.

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:30.960
Doctor said I need to
move my son out of here.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:34.631
He told me that he was
having trouble breathing,

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:38.635
getting headaches. He was
experiencing skin irritation.

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:41.346
He started finding dead
pigeons on his front lawn,

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:44.015
which, as you can imagine,
is pretty alarming.

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:47.810
And it appears, based on
some scientists I spoke to

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.103
who looked at this issue,

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:53.107
that these were fumes
from hydrocarbon exposure.

00:03:53.107 --> 00:03:56.110
That plastic was being melted down,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.987
which of course creates fumes

00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:00.531
that if they're not
properly filtered and vented

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:04.410
can be really damaging
to people and wildlife.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.039
And so that's what Vinyou
was experiencing in his home.

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:09.958
So if this is killing pigeons,

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:11.584
it's pretty bad.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.336
It's going to kill my family too.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.506
So, I have to let it go.

00:04:17.507 --> 00:04:19.092
And leave this house?

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:20.927
Yes, leave the house.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:31.980
When I think of a recycling plant,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.982 position:59%
certainly, before I did this research,

00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:37.151
you think of something
clean and inoffensive,

00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:39.112 position:61%
you know, part of this circular economy

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:42.115
that we're all told is
such a wonderful thing.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.450 position:56%
But the reality is a little messier.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.830
Recycling plants can be quite
dirty and dangerous places.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.041 position:58%
They can throw off serious pollution,

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:54.585 position:64%
chemicals that include things like dioxin

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:57.380
which had been associated
with increased risk of cancer.

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.554
So we're heading out of Bangkok now

00:05:04.554 --> 00:05:06.514
to Chon Buri province,

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.725 position:56%
and we're going to visit a community

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:10.268 position:59%
where there's been a lot of opposition

00:05:10.268 --> 00:05:13.730
to plastic recycling and
manufacturing facilities.

00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:20.028
The company do not keep,
you know, follow the rule.

00:05:20.028 --> 00:05:21.362
Follow the right thing.

00:05:21.362 --> 00:05:25.241
Sometimes they just burn
the plastic, you know,

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:26.117
whenever they want.

00:05:26.117 --> 00:05:30.538
They put this under the
ground, pollute the water.

00:05:30.538 --> 00:05:33.583
You know, they run it all
the time making noises.

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.044
People cannot stand that.

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.465
Most of the people affected
by recycling plants,

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:42.717 position:56%
and this is true all over the world,

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:46.095 position:61%
tend to be poor, tend to be vulnerable,

00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:47.847 position:61%
people who don't have a lot of recourse

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:50.266
to the legal system or otherwise.

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.187 position:58%
We organize three protests, actually.

00:05:55.813 --> 00:05:57.607
Each one get bigger and bigger.

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:01.069
The last one was about
a year and a half ago

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:04.947
and we got together
right around 700 people

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:08.076 position:64%
right in front of the factory, the plant.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:10.995
We protest, we put up the sign,

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:15.249
but at the end, it's just
another activity that we did

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:17.210
but never get any response back.

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:27.804
There's a simple reason why

00:06:27.804 --> 00:06:30.932
there is such a huge demand
for recycling globally,

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:33.559
and that is that we all consume

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.771
incredible amount of
plastic, in particular.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:40.191
Plastic consumption has
skyrocketed all over the world.

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:44.487
But countries like the US,
like Japan, like the UK

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:46.406
don't have the domestic capacity

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:48.116
to recycle all that plastic.

00:06:48.116 --> 00:06:49.742
In fact, they have nowhere near

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:52.161 position:59%
the domestic capacity they would need.

00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:53.788
So, the only way to deal with it,

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:56.666
if you don't want to just
throw it into landfill,

00:06:56.666 --> 00:07:00.044
is to send it overseas, to stuff
it into shipping containers

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:02.046
and send it to poorer countries

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:03.923 position:58%
where that infrastructure does exist.

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:05.299
So, that's what we've seen

00:07:05.299 --> 00:07:07.677
over the last five or six
years in Southeast Asia

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:12.640
is a big increase in
long-haul recycling shipments

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:14.183
largely from the rich world.

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:23.359
We detected a container full
of so-called plastic scraps.

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:25.945
But, you know, you opened it
up, it's not scraps inside,

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.864
it's all plastic waste.

00:07:27.864 --> 00:07:28.698
This is recently?

00:07:28.698 --> 00:07:30.241
Yeah, a couple months ago.
Oh, really. OK.

00:07:30.241 --> 00:07:33.953 position:58%
So we kindly throw it back to the US.

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:37.457
I've asked our
pollution-control department

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:38.958
to coordinate very closely

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.335 position:59%
with all the other agencies concerned,

00:07:41.335 --> 00:07:45.465 position:61%
and put, well, put our fist down on it.

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:46.841
Of course we cannot just say

00:07:46.841 --> 00:07:50.052
we're going to stop from
100 to zero overnight.

00:07:50.052 --> 00:07:52.263
It has to be a gradual change,

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:54.515 position:56%
and, you know, there are differences

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:58.436 position:64%
between plastic scraps and plastic waste.

00:07:58.436 --> 00:08:03.191
Unfortunately, the plastic
waste are being disguised

00:08:03.191 --> 00:08:05.651
as plastic scraps on paper.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.154
And in the past, I think
some people have used it

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:12.867
as a loophole to bring
those waste into Thailand.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.871
The Thai government
has for a few years now

00:08:16.871 --> 00:08:21.667
been moving toward a ban on
foreign plastic waste import.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:24.128
But as I learned from
the environment minister

00:08:24.128 --> 00:08:26.005
and from others I've spoken to,

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.798
that's much easier said than done.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:30.801
Recycling is a big industry.

00:08:30.801 --> 00:08:34.138
There are, in Thailand,
Malaysia, Indonesia,

00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:35.932
large and profitable companies

00:08:35.932 --> 00:08:38.059
that depend on, among other things,

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:39.977 position:56%
plastic waste imports from overseas.

00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:42.772
And so, they've been
a source of opposition

00:08:42.772 --> 00:08:43.814
to these measures.

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:45.858
Do you think the government

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:49.529
will really ban plastic
imports effectively?

00:09:20.434 --> 00:09:21.894
Over the last several years,

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:24.021
Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand,

00:09:24.021 --> 00:09:26.941
those countries have cracked
down to varying extents

00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:28.734
on waste imports.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:32.947
And what's going to happen
next, I think it's very fair to

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:35.992
say, is these flows of waste

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:38.077 position:63%
will find their ways to other countries,

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:41.205
and perhaps to countries
that are much poorer.

00:09:41.205 --> 00:09:43.916
Myanmar is one that
gets talked about a lot.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:45.084
There are countries in Africa

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:46.335
that are being talked about a lot

00:09:46.335 --> 00:09:48.087
as potential destinations.

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:51.966
And these are places even
less able to cope with these

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:54.760
inflows than a Thailand
or an Indonesia is.

